  <h1>Where these dates come from</h1>

  {#- Count both lists: the weekly featured days and the also-celebrated days on the grid. -#}
  {%- set alsoCount = animalDays.alsoCelebrated | length %}
  <div class="ab-stat"><b>{{ (animalDays.days | length) + alsoCount }}</b><span>animal days a year: {{ officialCount + alsoCount }} real awareness days and {{ pickCount }} Wild Atlas picks.</span></div>

  <h2>We didn't make them up</h2>
  <p>Every awareness day on the calendar was started by someone else. They come from different places:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The United Nations declared some of them, like <a href="https://wildlifeday.org/en/about" rel="noopener">World Wildlife Day</a> and <a href="https://www.un.org/en/observances/oceans-day/background" rel="noopener">World Oceans Day</a>.</li>
    <li>Conservation groups, zoos and aquariums started many more, to help people care about one animal at a time. For example, <a href="https://www.internationalleopardday.org/" rel="noopener">International Leopard Day</a> was started by the Cape Leopard Trust, Panthera and Saving the Survivors, and <a href="https://www.acap.aq/world-albatross-day/introduction-to-world-albatross-day" rel="noopener">World Albatross Day</a> by the international agreement that protects albatrosses.</li>
    <li>Animal fans started a few. <a href="https://tonmo.com/threads/its-world-octopus-day.94241/" rel="noopener">World Octopus Day</a> began in 2007 with an online community of octopus fans, who picked the 8th for an animal with eight arms.</li>
    <li>A few are United States days. <a href="https://www.wcs.org/get-involved/updates/senate-passes-bison-day-resolution" rel="noopener">National Bison Day</a> was started in 2012 by a coalition of tribes, ranchers and conservationists, and the U.S. Senate has recognised it every year since 2013.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When they happen</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Most fall on the same date every year.</li>
    <li>A few move around, like "the third Friday in May". The calendar works out the right date for each year automatically.</li>
    <li>Each day's page says where the day came from, with a link to its source.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How an animal gets on the calendar</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>One featured animal a week, matched to a real awareness day.</li>
    <li>When several animal days land in the same week, we feature the one we think kids will love most. The others still show on the calendar as "also celebrated" days.</li>
    <li>In the few weeks with no animal day, we choose a favourite and label it a <span class="pick-badge">Wild Atlas pick</span>.</li>
    <li>The year ends with Explorer's Choice.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What's on each page</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Featured animals get a short story to read out loud together, how big and how heavy the animal is next to a five-year-old, what its babies look like, and a map of where to see one, at zoos and aquariums and in the wild.</li>
    <li>Also-celebrated days get a shorter page: where the day came from, what it celebrates, why it matters, and a few fun facts.</li>
    <li>Facts are checked against trusted sources, like the <a href="https://www.iucnredlist.org/" rel="noopener">IUCN Red List</a> and the zoos, aquariums and groups behind each day.</li>
  </ul>

